WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.460
OpenAI is adding invisible digital watermarks to ChatGPT text, so people can spot when AI wrote something. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 188. You know that uneasy pause when a teacher or a boss wonders who really type those words? That pause is why openAI is putting hidden markers, called text watermarks, on some writing from ChatGPT and its other tools. People cannot see those markers.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:35.060
features. Special software can. Open AI announced. Picture a secret stamp that only a scanner notices.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.700
The stamp does not change how the sentences look or feel. You still read the same essay,

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:47.220
caption, or story. Nothing extra pops onto the page. The goal is simple. Help someone

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:52.600
tell whether a piece of writing probably came from an open AI model. A model here just

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.400
means the writing system behind the chatbot. It learned patterns from huge amounts of

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.760
text. That check is getting more useful as students, writers, and everyday users create

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.420
more content with these tools. School papers, hobby stories, and social posts all sit in

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:14.060
that mix now. The change starts first in Europe to follow new rules about artificial intelligence

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:20.320
or AI, transparency. Transparency here means being more open about when a machine helped

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.940
write something. The markers will roll out on chat GPT text and on Codex text over the

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:32.480
coming weeks. Codex is another open AI writing tool. Same idea. Hidden markers in the words

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.440
it produces. For you, that can mean a teacher, a contest judge, or a platform could one day

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:44.980
check whether AI helped. If you use chat GPT on homework, a story, or a social post, that

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:50.700
check might exist. It can feel a little strange at first. It also makes the setup more honest.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.780
Honest labeling can ease worries about fake or misleading writing floating around.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.760
Right now the detector is only open to approved researchers so they can test and improve it.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.380
You cannot run that scanner yourself yet.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.780
Researchers are still shaking it down.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.380
The bigger idea is more choice and more clarity about what these tools can and cannot do.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.180
If you want open AI's own explanation, go to OpenAI's website and look up their post

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.480
on text watermarks.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.880
Read how they describe the stamp, who can detect it, and what they are still unsure about.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.440
The part that still confuses people is how a watermark can hide inside ordinary sentences

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:30.980
at all.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.460
Your phone already guesses the next word when you text, based on patterns from millions

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:37.120
of messages.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.560
Now picture that same guesser, lightly sprinkling invisible glitter across the whole note.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.760
The glitter is not extra letters or weird symbols.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.240
You would never see sparkles in the paragraph.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.900
It is a tiny statistical nudge.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.320
At certain moments, the system leans toward one OK word over another OK word.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:02.140
Statistical just means it is playing the odds, the way auto-complete already does, only

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:03.460
with a faint rhythm added.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.740
Later, a detector hunts for that rhythm, the way special headphones might reveal a secret

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:09.600
beat in a song.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.880
The pattern lives in the choices the model makes while it writes, not in a tag stuck

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:15.540
on the end.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:21.380
That is why rewriting the text or translating it can wash the glitter away, like rinsing

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.240
a shirt until the faint dye fades.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:29.100
Open AI is being careful about how far it trusts the system right now, because washing

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:29.860
is possible.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:34.320
The watermark is not a name tag, and it is not a deed of ownership.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.380
It only offers a quiet clue about origin.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.500
Was this text likely made by one of our models?

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:46.020
Once you hear it as a hidden beat in word choice, the whole trick stops feeling like magic.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.480
While that rolls out, two new helpers landed that you can tap today, no coding needed.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.380
TikTok added a conversational AI agent inside its app that helps you shop.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:02.020
An agent here is a helper that chats with you and can take a next step, not just spit

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:02.720
out a paragraph.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:08.280
TechCrunch reports it can find products, answer questions about them, and let you check

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.220
out with one tap.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:14.360
app. Treat it like a shop clerk who already memorized the whole catalog and listens when

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:20.020
you describe what you want. Shopping on social apps is already a teen habit. This makes

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:25.380
the suggestions feel more like a back and forth. Anyone with the TikTok app can try it

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:31.260
right away. Open the app, go to the shopping section, and start typing. Try a line like,

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:37.640
show me cool sneakers under $50. Then watch how it answers and steers you. Notice whether

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.780
it asks a follow-up, shows options, or offers that one tap checkout.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.240
That is the agent doing work.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:49.920
Instinct is rolling out group chats where several people share one AI agent on a task.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:55.980
TechCrunch reports you can plan a trip, organize a carpool, or set up an event together in

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:56.500
that chat.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:59.020
Each person keeps a private account.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.540
The agent only shares information or takes action after everyone agrees.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:09.180
Think of a group project chat with an extra teammate who waits for a group yes before booking

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:09.680
anything.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:15.140
It is a way to watch an agent join a real conversation instead of working alone in

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:16.020
a private box.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.840
Visit the Instinct website or app and start a group chat.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.960
Friends can jump in even without their own accounts.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:27.980
Pick something small like a weekend hangout and see how the agent waits for agreement

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:28.860
before it moves.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.340
Hacker Rank built an AI interviewer that has already run over 500,000 practice sessions.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.940
Companies like Snowflake and Cap Gemini are testing it, TechCrunch reports.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:46.680
Students and job seekers get a low pressure way to rehearse answers and receive feedback before a real interview.

